# useEffect and Dependencies

Now that we can run an effect, the next question is how to control *when* that effect runs. That is the job of the **dependency array**, the optional second argument to `useEffect`.

```jsx
React.useEffect(() => {
  document.title = 'Count: ' + count;
}, [count]);
```

An effect always runs once after the component mounts. After that, React re-runs it whenever any value in the dependency array changes. The effect above depends on `count`, so React runs it again every time `count` changes.

## The Three Dependency Patterns

The second argument controls re-runs, and there are three distinct cases depending on what you pass.

**1. No dependency array.** If you leave the second argument out entirely, the effect runs after *every* render.

```jsx
React.useEffect(() => {
  console.log('runs after every render');
});
```

**2. An empty array, `[]`.** The effect runs once after the first render (the mount) and never re-runs because of state or prop changes. This is the pattern to use when you want to do something a single time, such as fetching a config file when the component appears.

```jsx
React.useEffect(() => {
  // fetch a config file once when the component mounts
}, []);
```

**3. An array with values, `[a, b]`.** The effect runs after the first render, and again whenever any listed value changes. React compares each value to its previous value by identity, the same way it compares state.

```jsx
React.useEffect(() => {
  // runs on mount, then whenever `a` or `b` changes
}, [a, b]);
```

### The Rule of Thumb

As a general rule of thumb, include every reactive value referenced inside the effect in the dependency array. A reactive value is one that can change between renders, such as a state variable, a prop, or something derived from them. If your effect reads `count`, `count` belongs in the array; if it reads a `user` prop, that prop belongs in the array. As with state, we do not mutate dependency values, because React decides whether they changed by comparing object identity.

## A Runnable Example

This counter updates the browser tab title whenever `count` changes, because `count` is listed as a dependency.

```{react}
function CounterTitle() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    document.title = 'Count: ' + count;
  }, [count]);

  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>;
}

renderReact(<CounterTitle />);
```

- The effect writes to `document.title`, which is a side effect on the page outside of React.
- `count` is referenced inside the effect, so it appears in the dependency array `[count]`.
- Each click updates `count`, which triggers a re-render, and because `count` changed, React runs the effect again with the new value.

## Two Effects, Two Dependencies

When a component has more than one piece of state, giving each effect the right dependencies keeps them independent. Here the title effect only reacts to `name`, and the dark-mode effect only reacts to `dark`. Typing in the input does not run the dark-mode effect, and toggling dark mode does not run the title effect.

```{react}
function Settings() {
  const [name, setName] = React.useState('');
  const [dark, setDark] = React.useState(false);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    document.title = 'Welcome ' + (name || 'guest');
  }, [name]);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (dark) {
      document.body.classList.add('dark');
    } else {
      document.body.classList.remove('dark');
    }
  }, [dark]);

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} placeholder="Your name" />
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox" checked={dark} onChange={(e) => setDark(e.target.checked)} /> Dark mode
      </label>
      <p>Hello {name || 'guest'}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

renderReact(<Settings />);
```

- The first effect depends on `[name]`, so it only re-runs when the typed name changes.
- The second effect depends on `[dark]`, so it only re-runs when the checkbox toggles.
- If we had omitted both dependency arrays, each effect would run after every render, no matter which value changed.

## Exercises

1. If you leave the dependency array out of a `useEffect` call entirely, how often does the effect run?

   ```{mcq}
   If you omit the dependency array entirely (pass no second argument), how often does the effect run?
   correct: Correct. With no dependency array, the effect runs after every render.
   wrong: Compare the three patterns: no array, an empty array, and an array with values.
   [x] After every render.
   [ ] Once after the first render only.
   [ ] Never, because the effect is disabled.
   [ ] Only when the browser tab regains focus.
   ```

2. This effect reads `count` but the dependency array is empty, so the title stops matching the count after the first render. Add `count` to the dependency array so the effect re-runs on every change.

   ```{react}
   function CounterTitle() {
     const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

     React.useEffect(() => {
       document.title = 'Count: ' + count;
     }, []);

     return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>;
   }

   renderReact(<CounterTitle />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function CounterTitle() {
     const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

     React.useEffect(() => {
       document.title = 'Count: ' + count;
     }, [count]);

     return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>;
   }

   renderReact(<CounterTitle />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     wrap(source.includes('[count]')).should('equal', true).run('Add `count` to the dependency array', 'Added the dependency');
     assert('button').click().run('Click the counter', 'Clicked the counter');
     // The state update re-renders asynchronously too, so give it a tick.
     setTimeout(function () {
       assert('button').should('have.text', 'Count: 1').run('The counter should still update', 'Counter updated');
     }, 50);
   }, 50);
   ```

3. An effect reads a `user` prop and a `page` state variable inside its callback. According to the rule of thumb, what should its dependency array contain?

   ```{mcq}
   An effect's callback references both a `user` prop and a `page` state variable. What should its dependency array be?
   correct: Correct. Include every reactive value the effect references, so both `user` and `page` belong in the array.
   wrong: The rule of thumb is to include every reactive value (state or props) referenced inside the effect.
   [x] `[user, page]`
   [ ] `[]`, because effects should run only once.
   [ ] `[user]` only, because props never change.
   [ ] No array at all, so it always stays in sync.
   ```

